U.S. Customs and Border Protection  
National Commodity Specialist Division  
New York, NY

**RE: Request for a binding country of origin ruling — 12 VDC electric actuator**

Dear Sir or Madam:

Pursuant to 19 C.F.R. Part 177, [REQUESTER NAME] respectfully requests a binding ruling on the country of origin of 12 VDC electric actuator for purposes of marking, determined under the Part 102 rules of origin (the general rules of 19 C.F.R. § 102.11, applied in order, with the tariff shifts of § 102.20).

CBP previously ruled on the current production setup: origin China (CBP ruling N348465, 5 June 2025). This request concerns a proposed change to that setup: move the motor stator and rotor to Canada.

## Facts

The DC motor is made in Canada. The gearbox, screw drive and housings are made in China. Final assembly in Canada.

## Issue

What is the country of origin of 12 VDC electric actuator, produced as described above, under the Part 102 rules of origin (the general rules of 19 C.F.R. § 102.11, applied in order, with the tariff shifts of § 102.20), for purposes of country of origin marking (19 C.F.R. § 102.0)?

This request concerns the origin of the merchandise for marking under the Part 102 rules only. It does not ask CBP to decide the country of origin for Section 301 duties, which is a separate question, and the requester does not contend that marking origin settles it.

## Requester's position

The requester submits that the merchandise is a product of Canada, for marking under the Part 102 rules, based on these key factors: motor made in Canada. [REQUESTER TO CONFIRM AND EXPAND WITH COUNSEL.]

Tariff-shift check (19 C.F.R. § 102.20): applied to the component codes in the bill of materials, with the good finished in Canada, the rule gives Canada (19 C.F.R. § 102.11(a)(3); 19 C.F.R. § 102.20(p), 8501). [CONFIRM WITH COUNSEL]

## Earlier CBP rulings considered

- CBP ruling N340187 — a comparable change where CBP found the origin moved ([rulings.cbp.gov](https://rulings.cbp.gov/ruling/N340187)).

